## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its Security/CryptoKit frameworks. .NET's `DSA` class on macOS delegates to the OS crypto libraries, so once that platform support was dropped, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. This change was deferred through .NET 10 but took effect in .NET 11 as the obsolete Apple SecTransform API path was finally removed.

## How to fix it

You have two options, depending on whether you can move off DSA:

### Option 1 — Migrate away from DSA (recommended)
DSA (FIPS 186-2) is considered legacy. If you control both signing and verification, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** (`SignData`/`VerifyData` via `ECDsa.Create()`), which are supported on all three platforms.

### Option 2 — Use OpenSSL on macOS for legacy verification
If you must keep verifying existing DSA signatures, use the **OpenSSL interop** shim so you aren't dependent on Apple's crypto stack:

1. Install OpenSSL on macOS (e.g. `brew install openssl`).
2. Use `DSAOpenSsl` (from `System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl`):

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

DSA dsa;
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    // Use OpenSSL provider directly, bypassing Apple frameworks
    dsa = new DSAOpenSsl();
}
else
{
    dsa = DSA.Create();
}

dsa.ImportParameters(legacyParams);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
```

3. Ensure the OpenSSL dylib is discoverable at runtime (set `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` or install to a standard location).

### Summary

| | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **Migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519** | Future-proof, no extra deps | Requires re-signing existing data |
| **DSAOpenSsl on macOS** | No re-signing needed | Adds OpenSSL runtime dependency on macOS |

If you only need to **verify** old signatures (not create new ones), Option 2 is the lowest-friction path. For new work, prefer ECDSA.
